00 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434312 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc1ebd9a-4d55-5ce2-ab52-fd21dda2062f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1f/1f62f15382a5f5d91ea1e2f56acc973d009316f62bdfd70f61fe131a28e42da1.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of rhombuses to the total number of shapes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c30cede-8611-5f0a-ae18-9d59bfca44eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434313 #edb/ref 1074 "We count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $4$ rhombuses.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $8$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of rhombuses to total shapes is $4:8.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of rhombuses to the total shapes is $1:2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2f/2fd396c9a62f29969acd4af3de44ac59dd4851a31b77e0c7902ab2e023942e31.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of heptagons to squares is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ heptagon for every $\\boxed{}$ squares." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b912b09-6665-5b0d-812c-febdf9d9cbee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434314 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we count the number of heptagons. We see that there are $4$ heptagons.\n\nThen, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $4$ squares.\n\nSo the ratio of heptagons to squares is $4:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $4:4.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of heptagons to squares is $1:1$ because there is $1$ heptagon for every $1$ square.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio $9:2$ is equivalent to the ratio $36:${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9df9f344-2897-5bbd-9091-a42a8b5a698c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434315 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the ratio equivalent to $9:2$, we multiply both numbers by the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,9 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,36 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get $36$ from $9,$ we multiply $9$ by $4.$ So, to get $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ from $2,$ we multiply $2$ by $4$ as well:\n\n$$\n4\\times 2=8\n$$\n\nThe ratio $9:2$ is equivalent to $36:8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio $12:15$ is equivalent to the ratio{{field-1}}$:5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d90a8def-3584-5231-870a-71215d7b2ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434316 #edb/ref 1074 "To find a ratio that's equivalent to $12:15,$ we divide both numbers by the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,15 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\boxed{} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get $5$ from $15,$ we divide $15$ by $3.$ So, to get $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ from $12,$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n12÷3=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:15$ is equivalent to $4:5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/79/79b659403c088680490bf308191be82b4acde242292210d15c1fb8525c7addfb.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of squares to the total number of shapes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f79f6f3f-0665-5958-9f52-a760bcfd2aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434317 #edb/ref 1074 "We count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $9$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of circles to total shapes is $3:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of squares to the total shapes is $1:3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1071 "A total of $25$ children and $10$ adults gathered for a charity event. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of children to adults is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there were $5$ children for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ adults." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f982038d-d23d-5345-b398-01e403a579af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434318 #edb/ref 1074 "Since there are $25$ children and $10$ adults, the ratio of children to adults is $25:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,25 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:2$ is equivalent to $25:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of children to adults is $5:2\\,$ because there were $5$ children for every $2\\,$ adults.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
